{# Plain-text limitations (single source). One paragraph per analysis: what it cannot conclude. #}
Framing. Everything in this report is a research hypothesis, not a treatment recommendation. No result here should change clinical care without clinical-grade confirmation and review by the child's clinical team.

Clinical features. The phenotype used here is the HPO set released to hackathon participants, which is not the complete clinical record. Absence of a feature from that set is not evidence of its absence in the child, so no negative phenotype claim is made anywhere in this report, and conclusions that would depend on a feature being genuinely absent are stated conditionally. A reader with access to the full record may be able to resolve those conditionals; this analysis cannot.

Pharmacogenomics, panel coverage. The pharmacogene panel is the 17 genes listed in the methods plus HLA-A, HLA-B and MT-RNR1. Genes bearing on this child's chemotherapy that lie outside it were not genotyped at all and are named in the report's panel-gap table; no result in this report speaks to them. Pharmacogenomics. Calls come from short-read WGS of blood with research tools; clinical pharmacogenetic testing uses validated assays. Allele-definition versions differ between callers (PharmCAT, Aldy, PyPGx), so a disagreement can reflect definition coverage rather than genotype. Two genes (VKORC1, IFNL3) cannot be compared between callers without assumptions. HLA typing is 2-field from short reads; class II is single-caller. Rare or novel alleles absent from the reference definitions are reported as undocumented variation, not interpreted. MT-RNR1 was measured in blood; heteroplasmy can differ between blood and the cochlea, and reads from nuclear mitochondrial segments (NUMTs) can add low-level apparent heteroplasmy, which is why allele fractions below the heteroplasmy threshold are reported but not called. The clinical contexts marked 'syndrome' (antiepileptic therapy, anaesthesia, supportive care) are inferred from the published MVA1 series, not from this child's record. Open Targets sources its pharmacogenomics from ClinPGx (formerly PharmGKB), the same knowledge base CPIC and PharmCAT draw on, so agreement between the Open Targets layer and PharmCAT is not independent corroboration. Annotations reflect curation at one release; a drug without PGx data is reported as no data, not as evidence of no effect.

Modifier panel. Small variants come from the distributed variant-only VCF; a position without a record may be reference or uncovered. Frequencies use the gnomAD joint population and group-maximum rather than an ancestry-matched subset. SpliceAI and AlphaMissense are predictions; SpliceAI does not score variants outside the MANE transcript bodies. Sensitivity for structural variants is limited for single-exon events, for balanced events with breakpoints in repeats, and for mosaic CNVs below roughly 30% of cells. A negative finding is bounded by these limits, and a modifier effect would need functional testing.

Mosaic aneuploidy. Blood may not reflect the tissues driving the phenotype, and aneuploidy levels in MVA vary between tissues and over time; this is one tissue at one time point. Sensitivity depends on coverage depth and the number of informative heterozygous sites per arm; per-arm detection limits are reported, and a non-significant arm means below that limit, not absent. Arm-level testing is insensitive to random, non-clonal aneuploidy spread across chromosomes, the characteristic MVA pattern, so bulk WGS cannot serve as a sensitive pharmacodynamic readout of it. Single sample, no panel of normals; profile confidence intervals assume independent heterozygous sites. No karyotype or cytogenetic result was available for cross-checking.

Genome QC. Ancestry is assigned to 1000 Genomes superpopulations only. Runs of homozygosity use a default allele frequency rather than population-specific frequencies. The sex check infers chromosomal sex from sequencing data only.

Secondary findings. The analysis was scoped to the panels described and did not screen for ACMG secondary findings. Two pharmacogenes (RYR1, CACNA1S) are also on the ACMG secondary-findings list; they are reported only as pharmacogenes, with CPIC susceptibility classifications.
